{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation landed in a worktree; change pushed for review (number in a follow-up comment).\n\n## What changed\n\nNew `src/rebar/_store/lock_kernel.py` (163 LOC) — THE kernel-mediated exclusive leg, one\nselector all three store write-lock probes route through. Contract is total: a real leg is\ntaken, or `NoExclusiveLegError` is raised and the caller never reaches the mkdir leg.\n\n- POSIX leg: `fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB)`, contention set `{EAGAIN, EACCES}` — the\n  same call and the same discrimination as before, verbatim.\n- Windows leg: `msvcrt.locking(fd, LK_NBLCK, 1)` after anchoring the offset, contention set\n  `{EACCES, EAGAIN, EDEADLK}`.\n\nCall sites rewired (the ONLY executable changes outside the new module):\n\n| file | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lock.py:207` `_acquire_fcntl` | `fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_EX\\|LOCK_NB)` | `_kernel.take_exclusive(fd)` |\n| `lock.py` same | `exc.errno not in (EAGAIN, EACCES)` | `not _kernel.is_contention(exc)` |\n| `lock.py:254` `write_lock_is_busy` | `fcntl.flock(...)` | `_kernel.take_exclusive(fd)` + a `NoExclusiveLegError -> return False` branch (it is advisory and documented to fail OPEN) |\n| `doctor_locks.py:142/148` `_probe_fcntl` | `fcntl.flock` / `LOCK_UN` | `_kernel.take_exclusive` / `release_exclusive` + `NoExclusiveLegError -> STATE_UNKNOWN` |\n\nEverything else in `lock.py` is docstring.\n\n## AC2 — the `fcntl_held=True` proof, rewritten rather than copied\n\n`fcntl_held=True` stays TRUE under the Windows leg, and the argument is its own. The POSIX\nproof rests on `flock` being kernel-mediated on the INODE (hence shared across pid/mount\nnamespaces on one kernel) and dropped when its holder dies — neither clause transfers to a\nplatform with no inodes and no pid namespaces. The Windows argument: a byte-range lock lives\non the kernel's file object rather than in the process, so it is enforced against every other\nhandle machine-wide; and the kernel releases it when the owning handle closes, which it does\nfor every handle on process termination, abnormal included. Same two properties the\nreclamation argument needs, earned differently. Written out in `lock_kernel`'s module\ndocstring, in `_acquire_mkdir`'s precondition, and in `_mkdir_lock_is_stale`'s parameter doc.\n\n## Adjacent Windows hazard found and fixed in the same path\n\n`lock_owner._pid_alive` probes with `os.kill(pid, 0)`. Python documents that on Windows\n`os.kill` understands only `CTRL_C_EVENT`/`CTRL_BREAK_EVENT` and **TerminateProcess**-es the\ntarget for any other value, 0 included. That probe runs on every mkdir-lock contention, so on\nWindows reclamation would have KILLED the live writer it was asking about. Narrowed via\n`_signal_zero_probes_liveness()`: where signal 0 is not a probe there is no liveness signal,\nso the answer is the conservative one and the decision falls through to the branches that need\npositive proof (the held leg, or the age ceiling). Strictly narrower — nothing is reclaimed on\nless evidence than before.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**RED first, for the right reason.** Before the fix, with `fcntl` hidden, all three use sites\nraised `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'` — the exact sweep symptom.\n\n**POSIX unchanged, measured not asserted.** `test_concurrency_regression.py` +\n`test_store_concurrency.py` (storm) + `test_reads_under_sync_contention.py`: **39 passed**\nbefore the change, **39 passed** after. Broader lock/store sweep after: 693 passed, 11 skipped.\n`make lint` 0, `make typecheck` 0, complexity ratchet `new=0 increased=0`, mechanism ratchet\n`new=0 increased=0` (no new lock class, no new `.lock` filename).\n\n**Mutation — 7 mutants, 7 killed.**\n\n| # | mutant | killed by |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | `take_exclusive` degrades silently instead of raising (THE unsafe two-line fix) | `test_no_leg_acquire_refuses_loudly_and_never_takes_the_mkdir_leg`, `test_no_leg_doctor_probe_reports_unknown` |\n| M2 | Windows leg is a no-op (leg \"taken\", nothing held) | `test_windows_leg_exclusion_survives_the_mkdir_leg_being_removed`, `..._doctor_probe_answers_truthfully` |\n| M3 | negated `msvcrt` selection branch | 8 tests |\n| M4 | pid-probe narrowing removed | `test_pid_probe_never_signals_where_signal_zero_terminates` |\n| M5 | busy probe fails CLOSED | `test_no_leg_busy_probe_fails_open` |\n| M6 | doctor probe claims FREE with no leg | `test_no_leg_doctor_probe_reports_unknown` |\n| M7 | POSIX contention set narrowed (EACCES dropped) | **survived at first** — see below |\n\nM7 exposed a real pre-existing gap: nothing covered EACCES contention, only EAGAIN, so\ndropping it from the set turned a merely-contended lock into a hard write failure unnoticed.\nClosed by adding two cases to the EXISTING harness `tests/unit/test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`\n(`_acquire_fcntl` and the busy probe under EACCES). M7 is killed with them.\n\nNote M2 was NOT caught by the plain two-acquirer test — the mkdir leg alone still excludes.\nIt is caught by the `dual_window=False` variant, which isolates the kernel leg. That test is\nthe actual teeth behind the `fcntl_held` proof.\n\n## AC6 in-session check\n\n`grep -nE '(^|[^_[:alnum:]])fcntl\\.' src/rebar/_store/lock.py src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py\nsrc/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py` matches only prose. An AST census of every\n`fcntl.<attr>` access across `src/rebar` confirms zero remain in those three files; the only\nones on the write-lock path are `lock_kernel.py:124/146`, both inside `if fcntl is not None:`.\n\n## What is deliberately NOT in this change\n\n- `reducer/marker.py` — same defect, different lock. Existing bug `discrete-unrelative-vervet`\n  (`a50a-cac1-af18-42d9`). (I filed a duplicate before finding it; closed as duplicate.)\n- `_store/fsutil.sibling_exclusive_lock` — function-local `import fcntl`, so\n  `ModuleNotFoundError` at CALL time on the HLC-stamp write path. These are the 4\n  `ModuleNotFoundError` lines in the sweep census. It is a BLOCKING acquire and needs a\n  blocking take this selector does not expose. Filed as `hyperbolic-humanoid-owl`\n  (`658e-a99b-a004-4be3`), a child of the epic, `depends_on` this story.\n- `llm/gate_admission.py` — looks unguarded at `:233`/`:259`, but its public entry point\n  early-returns at `:319` on `fcntl is None`, so those lines are unreachable. No work.\n\n## Windows-native proof is deferred to CI by design\n\n`test_native_lock_is_visible_across_processes` and `test_native_lock_dies_with_its_holder`\nrun only where `os.name == 'nt'` and skip on this host. They are the only place the real\n`msvcrt.locking`'s two properties are established; the POSIX-host simulation proves rebar's\nBRANCH (the stub delegates to the host's `flock`, so exclusivity is real), not Microsoft's\nkernel. Those two tests execute in the epic's `sweep (windows)` job.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788588101423926794,"uuid":"2f7774c3-8c0c-4879-805a-ee536238e88f"}